<Callout kind="warn">
The original #4471 page says “one issue, one fix queued.” Its rendered data shows two causes.
Known does not mean fixed, and a parser fix does not repair the booking-v3 date hint.
This review replaces that diagnosis, not the historical report export.
</Callout>

## The split

<ReportAudit />

These counts come from unique report IDs, restricted to `mergedInto === 4471`, then
`groupByCause` and the tenant directory. The classifier asks `Intl.DateTimeFormat` for each
tenant's date order. Browser language, intake guesses, and matching subjects do not determine
the cause. The export is a snapshot through 4 October 2026, not a live support queue.

### Parser: dates are read month first

The shared parser treats numeric dates as month/day even when a tenant writes day/month.
The result is a month/day swap. For example, `R-41200` is an app report from
`harbour.dental` (`en-GB`, `booking-v2`): a 7 April reminder went out on 4 July.

The supplied classifier assigns this cause to day-first tenants on app and email,
and to forms other than the booking-v3 hint path. Fix the parser using the tenant's
date convention; test ambiguous dates, invalid dates, and day-first/month-first controls.
Do not infer that any fix has shipped from the word “queued.”

### Hint: booking-v3 tells people the wrong input format

The booking-v3 field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but reads the value using the day-first tenant's
format. People follow the hint and their booking lands on the wrong date. This is a
different path and needs its own fix: generate the hint and input handling from the same
tenant convention.

For example, `R-41230` is a form report from `copper.studio` (`es-ES`, `booking-v3`).
Intake tagged it `parser`, but the tenant/form/channel combination classifies it as `hint`.
The affected form tenants in this export are `alder.physio`, `copper.studio`, and
`pale.harbour`. Test them separately from app/email paths and booking-v2.

## What the existing page actually renders

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

“Tagged as” records intake's guess, not the computed diagnosis. The table's minority
cause is marked “not this ticket — no ticket of its own.” That is why the two product
problems need separate GitHub issues.

## What the timeline can and cannot establish

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports received in April and merged in July were not first received in July.
The chart separates arrival from administrative merging. It does not prove the exact
time either bug was introduced, fixed, or deployed.

## Keep the desk from merging them again

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) remains unchanged as history.
The [replacement decision](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) proposes cause-based
triage. The interactive example below already uses cause matching, although the old
triage prose says to merge by subject alone.

<DedupeExplorer />

- Same subject does not establish the same cause: `alder.physio` on form is `hint`,
  while the same tenant on app is `parser`.
- Different subjects can describe the same cause: app and email for `kettle.books`
  are both `parser`.
- Unknown tenants have no established cause. Keep them for investigation; do not
  merge two unknowns just because both lack a diagnosis.
- Recheck tenant locale, booking form, channel, and rendered evidence as the desk
  receives new reports. Update ticket counts by unique report ID, not by export row.

## Evidence and limits

The source is the 4 October export: `reports-4471.json`, `tenants/*.json`,
`desk-notes.json`, and the executable `causes.ts`, `locales.ts`, `CauseTable`,
`QueueTimeline`, and `DedupeExplorer`. The original prose and decision were last reviewed
in 2024 or September 2026; the shared layout warns when a review exceeds 180 days.
This page records an analysis and proposed triage change. It does not claim that either
product fix has shipped or that a production support queue has been changed.

